{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who has set a mouth for man</b> — speech, because it is in the mouth. And likewise, \"one language\" (Genesis 11:1), \"who speak the language of Canaan\" (Isaiah 19:18)." "<b>Or who makes mute</b> — they explained it there (Guide for the Perplexed III:10) as applying to man: \"or who makes a man mute,\" that is, who created man such that he is mute. And the הַשִּׂימָה (\"setting\") applies to man, for muteness is not \"set,\" since it is not a thing, but rather it is the absence of speech. But perhaps, since there is in man a speaking soul and the mute, when there is an obstruction in the tendons of the tongue, become mute, it may be possible to say \"who sets muteness.\" And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (ibid.) that one who removes an acquisition is said to have produced its removal, for they say of one who extinguishes the lamp that he brought darkness. And likewise according to his opinion: \"I form light and create darkness, I make peace and create evil\" (Isaiah 45:7)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The phrase הַשִּׂימָה לָאָדָם is rendered hyper-literally as 'the setting applies to man'; Ramban's point is that the verb שׂוּם ('set, place') grammatically attaches to אָדָם ('man') rather than to אִלֵּם ('mute'), since muteness is a privation, not a positive quality."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:36.896055684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}